v10  2026-10-22
Changed from v9: run_matrix.sh only.

  1. calibrate_binding() extracted the time with
         sed -n 's/.*total_us=\([0-9.]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1
     which returns an EMPTY STRING on every run. The harness prints total_us
     on two kinds of line; the first is the per-run line, formatted %10.2f, so
     `total_us=` is followed by spaces, `[0-9.]*` matches nothing, sed prints
     an empty line and head -1 returns it. Hence the v9 log line
         [CALIB] a calibration run produced no result -- leaving binding off
     The two runs themselves had worked. Now the number is read off the
     [RESULT] line only, by awk field, and a failure prints the tail of the
     calibration log instead of just giving up quietly.

  2. The calibration's two runs appended rows to output/matrix.csv, which had
     already been rotated -- so the v9 summary read "cells: 146  PASS=146"
     against "[DONE ] cells=144". The table sizes are now recorded before the
     calibration and cut back to them afterwards.

  3. The calibration cell is now the LARGEST N of the variant's list, not the
     smallest. At the small end the host stages are a few per cent of the
     runtime and the bound/unbound difference sits inside the noise, which
     makes the 5% decision a coin flip.

  4. The calibration variant now skips chk/* diagnostics, which write no
     output and would SKIP.

Nothing else changed: no kernel, no host code, no test. A v9 table is still
readable, it just has two spurious rows at the smallest N of the first
variant, tile=64 minT=8.
